# poolhound collectors on the server.  Installed to /etc/cron.d/poolhound.
#
# THE SAMPLER IS NOT HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT
# On the Mac a job polled the pool controller every 15 minutes. the server cannot do
# that and must not try: it has no route to the house LAN, and giving it one
# would put an unauthenticated controller on a routable path. The Pi's agent
# pushes samples up instead, so the 15-minute cadence now lives on the Pi.
#
# Times are UTC. See the block below for why they are not Pacific, and for the
# step in the daily series that discovering it explained.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
# TIMES ARE UTC, BECAUSE CRON_TZ DOES NOT WORK HERE.
#
# This file used to carry `CRON_TZ=America/Los_Angeles` above a comment
# promising the times matched what the Mac ran "so the daily series has no step
# in it across the migration". CRON_TZ is a cronie feature; the server runs Debian's
# vixie-cron, where the string does not occur in the binary at all
# (`grep -c CRON_TZ /usr/sbin/cron` -> 0). It was parsed as an ordinary
# environment assignment and never reached the scheduler, so every job ran at
# its stated hour in UTC — and the server is Etc/UTC.
#
# The collectors therefore fired at 07:00 Pacific, seven hours early, from the
# migration onward. The WaterGuru pod measures between 19:37 and 21:15 UTC, so
# a 14:00 UTC pull is always BEFORE that day's measurement: every scheduled run
# since 2026-09-13 fetched the PREVIOUS day's reading, spending the one API call
# a day this project says it respects on data it already had. Measured in
# readings.csv — fetched 2026-09-17T07:00:05-0700, measured 2026-09-16T19:38:04Z
# — against the Mac era, fetched 2026-09-12T20:00:40, measured the same day.
#
# 23:00 UTC is 16:00 PDT and 15:00 PST: comfortably after the latest measurement
# observed (21:15 UTC) in either half of the year, and the same calendar day in
# Pacific, which is what the daily series is keyed on. A fixed UTC time shifts
# an hour in local terms across DST and that is fine here; being after the pod
# is the property that matters, not the wall-clock hour.

# NOBODY WAS READING STDERR. MAILTO="" discards it, the server has no MTA so mail
# would have bounced anyway, and every job below pipes its failures into that
# nothing. bin/watch had been dying with a TypeError on EVERY run for weeks --
# the one job whose entire purpose is to tell you a collector has gone quiet --
# and this line is why nobody found out. The mail stays off, because it cannot
# work; the output goes to a file that does, and collect.sh records each run's
# exit status where --check can judge it.
MAILTO=""
POOLHOUND_LOG=/var/log/poolhound-cron.log
# The run log records WHO started an attempt. This is the one place that knows
# the answer is cron; everything else says "manual" rather than guessing from
# whether stdin happens to be a terminal.
POOLHOUND_RUN_BY=cron
# WHERE THE STACK IS, substituted by bootstrap-server.sh from server.env's STACK.
# cron.d assignments reach the shell that runs each command, so the lines below
# expand it. It used to be one install's absolute path written out four times,
# which made this repository's cron file a statement about one person's disk.
STACK=@@STACK@@

# THE TWO PULLS ARE TICKS NOW, NOT A SCHEDULE.
#
# These fired once a day from here, which meant "how often do you fetch
# WaterGuru" was a question the product could state and not answer: the
# container cannot edit a file on the host, and a web form that could rewrite
# /etc/cron.d would be a web form that can run anything as root. So the cadence
# moved to `[collection]` in config.toml -- which the container already owns,
# already writes, and which lives on the share -- and these lines became a
# heartbeat that asks.
#
# poolhound/cadence.py decides. `bin/wg-collect` calls cadence.tick_gate()
# BEFORE any network call and exits 0 having spent nothing when the answer is
# no, so an hourly tick against a two-calls-a-day budget costs exactly what a
# daily one did. The floor is enforced there rather than here, because a
# crontab cannot refuse a setting.
#
# HOURLY, AND THE HOUR STILL MATTERS. The cadence names the earliest hour a
# daily pull may land in, and its default is read from THIS FILE so that an
# install which never opens Settings keeps the hour it already had. 23:00 UTC
# is that hour for the reason set out above: the pod measures between 19:37 and
# 21:15 UTC and a pull before that fetches yesterday's reading every time.
#
# The minutes are kept apart so the two do not contend for the same lock.
0 * * * * root $STACK/poolhound/collect.sh wg-collect >> $POOLHOUND_LOG 2>&1

# Leslie's: in-store bench photometer results, scraped as a browser would.
5 * * * * root $STACK/poolhound/collect.sh leslies >> $POOLHOUND_LOG 2>&1

# Health and notifications: has a collector stopped, is the chemistry badly out,
# did a heater come on.
*/30 * * * * root $STACK/poolhound/collect.sh watch --quiet >> $POOLHOUND_LOG 2>&1

# A safety-net render. Samples and writes re-render inline, so this exists only
# to catch a day when nothing at all arrived — without it the page would still
# show yesterday's "x hours ago" as though it were current.
17 */6 * * * root $STACK/poolhound/collect.sh render >> $POOLHOUND_LOG 2>&1
# THE ONLY COPY OF THIS POOL'S HISTORY IS THE SHARE. A tarball onto the host's
# local disk is the one place guaranteed to fail separately from it — the .bak
# migrate_columns writes lives on the share beside the file it is backing up,
# so it is gone in exactly the case a backup is for. 40 past, so it does not
# collide with the labs at :00 and :05 or the render at :17.
40 2 * * * root $STACK/poolhound/collect.sh backup >> $POOLHOUND_LOG 2>&1
